n now. Possibly because in the previous turn I tried to play W8 but it wasn't my turn or something. Now it is valid. So I can choose it.  \n\nTo be safe, I'll choose W6 instead? But both are valid.  \n\nLet's evaluate more: My hand after discarding W8: W4, W5x2, W6, W7, T5, T6, T7, B5x2. That gives me potential chows: W4,5,6; W5,6,7; T5,6,7. And pairs: W5 and B5. Good flexibility.  \n\nIf I discard W6: W4, W5x2, W7, W8, T5, T6, T7, B5x2. Potential chows: W4,5,6? No W6. W5,6,7? No W6. W6,7,8? No W6. So I lose chow potential in W suit. So discarding W6 is worse.  \n\nTherefore, discard W8 is better.  \n\nSo my final choice: Play W8.  \n\nReason: W8 is the least connected tile in my hand given I have no W9, and discarding it preserves potential chows with W4,5,6 and W5,6,7, while maintaining pairs and T suit chow potential. It also keeps the hand flexible for achieving scoring patterns like All Chows and All Simples.  \n\nAnswer: Play W8.  \n\nResponse format:  \nEvaluation: Discarding W8 maintains hand flexibility and chow potential while being a safe discard.  \nReason: W8 is an edge tile with no adjacent W9, and keeping central tiles like W5 and W6 offers better sequence formation.  \nAnswer: Play W8"}
